WEBVTT

c0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Morai, Rabotai, ladies and gentlemen, the major thesis of this paper is to develop a kind of theory in which we could understand the role

c0002
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:47.000
of qabbala as a factor in apostasy, in the conversion of Jews in Spain. In order to understand the role of qabbala as a factor in apostasy, a few clarifications are of the essence.

c0003
00:00:47.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Our understanding of Judaism today, of rabbinic Judaism, is a little bit different than that of the Judaism in Spain prior to the rise of qabbala.

c0004
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:17.000
At that time, we had what we call the Maimonidean tradition. It is not a uniform tradition, but there are some very basic points, and those basic points later on through the rise of qabbala were questioned.

c0005
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Point number one, in the philosophy of Maimonides, Judah Levi, etc., the basis of the Tora is a covenant, is a historic covenant. The historic covenant results in misva, in commandments.

c0006
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Now, the most important aspect is that those misva, as Judah Levi defines them, are mazbutah. Each misva is mazbutah. Mazbutah means properly and accurately defined.

c0007
00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:08.000
So therefore, Judaism is committed not to a concept, but to a specific definition of the misva. Those definitions were the patrimony, not of individual scholars or individual rabbis, but rather of the bedin.

c0008
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:24.000
And it was a transmission from one court to another court. As Maimonides in the Mishne Tora explains, Tora she be'alpeh is mashem asar bedin, mipi bedin. It is a Jewish court.

c0009
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Now, this Jewish court, it was a national, and I underline national, institution of the Jews. With the termination of the end of the Talmudic period, 499, according to traditional sources, we no longer have a public bedin, but we have only individual bedin shel yachidim.

c0010
00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:06.000
That's what Maimonides says. Now, therefore, here comes a very important thing. Judaism, as Judah Levi says, is different than all other religions, and precisely these three points. It is the result of the covenant.

c0011
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:26.000
And because it is the result of the covenant, Judah Levi says, izhtihad, which means personal endeavor, sevara, conjecture, ideas, whether it comes from the heart or from the mind, according to Judah Levi and to the whole school, it is not part of the Jewish religion.

c0012
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Accordingly, otherwise, if you would accept izhtihad as a source for religion, then you cannot distinguish between Judaism, those who believe in polytheism, sorcerers, witchcraft, and all kinds of intellectual and spiritual aberrations.

c0013
00:03:42.000 --> 00:04:01.000
So point number one is, therefore, Judaism, it is not and cannot accept izhtihad as a source of spirituality. Number two, very important, whatever it is based upon izhtihad, therefore, it is abodah zara, abodah zara in the sense that it is idolatry.

c0014
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Not as Christianity interprets idolatry, and later on Jews interpret idolatry not as worshipping of idols, but as abodah shehi zara, a law or a kind of service to God, which is not prescribed by the law.

c0015
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:31.000
And therefore, according to Judah Levi, as he pointed out very clearly, the Jews were permitted to worship the keruvim at the temple, although there were images, because that was part of what the law prescribed.

c0016
00:04:31.000 --> 00:05:00.000
So the difference between abodah zara and non-abodah zara is precisely on the matter of izhtihad. Because we do not accept izhtihad, and therefore we do not accept religion as based upon personal opinion, therefore, another element, kias, which means judicial analogy, cannot be accepted as a source of law.

c0017
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Now, in view of the proceeding, we could appreciate much better the impact that the school of Catalonia and Girona, principally headed by the Ramban, the school of the Ramban, which Professor Ida was so kind to explain before to us, the impact of that school on Jewry in general, first in Catalonia, and then penetrated into Castile.

c0018
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Now, point, very important point of the Ramban. The Ramban, although the Tora says very clearly that the beddin is leidorotechem, v'chol mosheivotechem, means for generations, and in any place where you are established, the Ramban says very clearly, disagrees with my manners, and says that the Jewish court had ceased to operate, yes, with the destruction of the Second Temple,

c0019
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:14.000
which means the year 70 of the Common Era. A consequence of this theory, therefore, means that everything that the Talmud says, since it's post-Temple, it is not really the basis of a Jewish court of justice. This is a revolutionary idea presented by the Ramban.

c0020
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Again, according to the Ramban, the court of justice of the Jewish people ceased to function with the destruction of the Temple. Parenthesis, Spinoza in the Theological Political Treatise, he uses this in the introduction to say why he believes that there is no longer a Jewish nation, we no longer have a beddin, and therefore we cannot be possibly a nation.

c0021
00:06:37.000 --> 00:07:04.000
Now, since we do not have a beddin, then we can understand another point of the Ramban. The rabbis had no right to legislate from the Tora. The Tora has not given the rabbis the right to legislate. This is a focal point, a dispute between Ramban and the Rambam. According to the Rambam, all rabbinic legislation has its basis in the Tora.

c0022
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:19.000
But according to the Rambans, very clearly, legislation is no longer the orayta. We have no obligation from the Tora to obey or to listen or to comply with the rabbinic legislation.

c0023
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:44.000
So therefore, what is the source of rabbinic authority? Here comes something very interesting. It is not, obviously, a national institution like the Jews believed for generations from the time of the Geonim, etc., but it is because of a particular knowledge that the Rabbi or rabbis happen to have.

c0024
00:07:44.000 --> 00:08:03.000
And therefore Ramban says, what the rabbis have is not the right to legislate the Tora, but to explain the Tora. It is the explanation of the Rabbi and not the right or authority as a judicial body that gives them authority.

c0025
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Now, we have to come and see a very interesting consequence of it. It is a different kind of hermeneutics or theory of hermeneutics. I am not going to go into the details of it, but basically it is like this. If you study, and I have published several articles on the subject, in the Talmud, the method of hermeneutics of the rabbis is what I call that of the Stoics.

c0026
00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:45.000
That of the Stoics means we make connections. We take one term here and one term, another one, and we connect these two terms. And out of connecting these two terms, we generate some sort of an idea. Let me give you a simple example.

c0027
00:08:45.000 --> 00:09:07.000
We have the word le'obdo, to serve God. Now, literally, always to serve God in the Pentateuch means through sacrifice, through sacramental sacrifice. And then we have le'bavchem, heart. And then we connect these two terms. It says, ezo hi avodah shevalev, tzi tefilah. And then we invent or we establish or we create the notion of tefilah.

c0028
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:25.000
It is by connecting two different terms, exactly like the Stoics did it, that we generate a third idea. A and B produces C. Now, because of this, now you're going to understand another crucial point of Rambam and Ramban controversy.

c0029
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:49.000
According to the Rambam, everything that is learned through derasha, through hermeneutics, it is not the oraitah. It is not biblical. It is something which the rabbis generated. It is not the meaning of the verse, but it is something that the rabbis generated. That's the famous second principle of Rambam in the Sefer HaMitzvot.

c0030
00:09:49.000 --> 00:10:04.000
Everything which is learned through derasha, through hermeneutics, it is only the Ramban. Ramban disagrees with this and says, no. What is learned through hermeneutics, this is the real explanation of the Tora.

c0031
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:24.000
Now, here we come to what I call platonic concept of hermeneutics. There is a truth, a single truth, and then you reveal or discover this truth in the text. And once you discover that truth, that truth becomes the text.

c0032
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:47.000
To be very precise, that truth displaces the text. Or to be more precise even, once that you know the truth, then you rearrange the text in light of that truth and therefore the truth, yes, displaces the law or the text.

c0033
00:10:48.000 --> 00:11:05.000
It's very interesting. That's why Rambam says, and the argument of Rambam is the following. He says, if the derasha of the rabbis represent the truth, then there must be the oraitah. There must be the Bible. They cannot be second to the Bible. Is it the truth or is it not the truth?

c0034
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:23.000
Of course, from the school of my marriage, it's a question of authority. One was the result of a covenant and the other is the result of an authority given to the rabbis. According to Rambam, no. And here we come to a kind of exegesis hermeneutics which is really platonic and therefore it is Christian.

c0035
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:48.000
Example, the New Testament, yes, is the interpretation of the Old Testament, platonic. That's the main idea. And therefore, by being its interpretation, it displaces the Old Testament. That is the theory of hermeneutics behind the Christian approach to the Old Testament.

c0036
00:11:48.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Now, let me therefore come to a very interesting point. If the rabbis have no right to legislate, and if the rabbis are rabbis by virtue of knowledge, then how can we distinguish between one kind of knowledge, that of the rabbis, and another kind of knowledge, that of people who are not the rabbis?

c0037
00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:41.000
Here we come to another point, presented by the Rambam. The Rambam has a theory, which is, I want to mention another very interesting expression of the Rambam. There is a drashah of the Rambam, which according to Gershon Solem, it was written by the Rambam, and I believe that it was by the Rambam.

c0038
00:12:41.000 --> 00:13:01.000
Some people dispute it, but at any rate, even if it was not penned by the Rambam himself, it was written by one of his students. In that drashah, the Rambam refers to the Tora, the pshutah, the peshat of the Tora, as Tora rekanit, as something empty.

c0039
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:29.000
Yes? And says only through the interpretation, the mystical interpretation, then it becomes, you know, the valid or the true Tora. Now, the expression Tora rekanit, yes, it is within a Christian environment, it is very much a reminiscence of the famous distinction established by Paul between the letter of the law, which is davarek, and the spirit of the law.

c0040
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:51.000
Now, that distinction, introduced either by the Rambam or by a student of his, it is part of the whole idea that it is through a certain type of knowledge, a special knowledge, that the rabbis will transform the so-called Tora rekanit into a more spiritual type of system.

c0041
00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:07.000
Now, if the Tora is rekanit, then how do we know the true Tora? Another theory, very interesting, introduced by the Rambam, same school. In this, according to the theory, really Moses transmitted two kinds of Tora.

c0042
00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:23.000
One, the Tora, we have it written down in the text, in which the consonants are divided into words, and the words make up sentences. That type of a Tora, it was delivered to all the Jewish people.

c0043
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:47.000
But then there is another kind of Tora, in which the letters, all the consonants, are together. And these consonants, you divide according to some sort of esoteric system, which was not transmitted to the whole Jewish people, but somehow, it doesn't tell us how, it reached Gerona.

c0044
00:14:48.000 --> 00:15:16.000
And now, according to this way, then the Tora is Kol HaTorah Kula, are shemot, magical names of God. It is very interesting, the name of the shemot of God, because it also comes very close to the notion of logos, in which Jesus and the Tora according to the New Testament is somehow represented by the person of Jesus.

c0045
00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:39.000
Also you have in Islam, one of the theories in Israel is that the Koran is a dimension of God. And somehow, the names of this mystical, esoteric kind of a Tora, that only the Rabbis in Gerona, Catalonia, and southern France, they knew it somehow from Sinai to there, that is the real Tora.

c0046
00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:56.000
And now, these names, those names, are encoded throughout the Tora. And through this codification of names, Moses and other people were able to perform all the miracles.

c0047
00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:19.000
It is knowledge of those names, what gives the Rabbi the authority to explain, promulgate, and teach the Tora. Now, a few, to clarify, now some very radical ideas introduced by the Ramban, the schools, etc.

c0048
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:35.000
Now, one of the ideas is the following. We know that for instance, Maimonides was attacked, not Maimonides the person, but the writings, by the anti-Mammonidians.

c0049
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Now, in a book of mine, which just came out a couple of months ago, I tried to show that Ramban, of course, somehow also questions and attacks some of the rationalistic ideas attributed or said by the Maimonidian school.

c0050
00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:23.000
Now, normally when we imagine that the controversy between the Ramban and the Rambam, according to standard historiography, is that the Ramban somehow was more conservative in his religious ideology, and that the Rambam represented some sort of an alien or at least a rationalistic approach.

c0051
00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:41.000
But it is important to bear in mind that when the Ramban attacks the Rambam, the most crucial point that he attacks is Rambam's disbelief in demonology. Ramban did not believe in demons.

c0052
00:17:41.000 --> 00:18:07.000
Now, we're going to see why this was so important for the Ramban, the fact that the Rambam, Maimonides, rejects the notion of demons. And before we're going to analyze why it was so important for the Ramban that Rambam dismisses the whole notion of demons, the proof that the Ramban brings to show that the Rambam is wrong.

c0053
00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:36.000
And he says it is known on the basis of a science called necromancy that demons do exist. It's very important because here we see that it's not just, for instance, a verse in the Tora or a passage in the Talmud against the Rambam, but a whole system of thought.

c0054
00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:52.000
In which something is taken as a premise, in this case the science of necromancy, and he used the word necromancy, in which through the science of necromancy he says we know that demons do exist.

c0055
00:18:52.000 --> 00:19:15.000
As a matter of fact, he refers to the science of necromancy three times. But what is more important, and this is an interesting thing, is that this science of necromancy, he knows also, he speaks about Baal Shedim, people expert in the science of demonology.

c0056
00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:34.000
And Rambam says that he himself witnessed, he himself witnessed, and he says people who have this power, he uses demons, yes, to kill and to resurrect, to burn and to plant.

c0057
00:19:34.000 --> 00:20:02.000
And therefore says to deny this, it is irrational, and it is to go against the truth. Now, allow me for a parenthesis. This science of necromancy that he believed to be so important, and he attacked my mothers in several places.

c0058
00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:21.000
In one passage, an extraordinary passage, he says like this, he says, at one time when the Jews were in Egypt, even the Egyptians themselves and all the pagan world believed in demons.

c0059
00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:47.000
And it was only when that Greek came about, and he and his students, that for the first time the existence of demons was claimed. Now, normally, me included, when we study Tora, we always assume that that Greek, the Yevani, must be Aristotle.

c0060
00:20:48.000 --> 00:21:02.000
But as I showed in my Ionim, I think it was published in 1974, something like this, if you look into Aristotle, in no place Aristotle denies the existence of ghosts or demons.

c0061
00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:21.000
Moreover, there were works circulating in the Middle Ages, in Spain, among Jews, falsely, today we know that falsely attributed to Aristotle, in which Aristotle is supposed to believe like everybody else in demons.

c0062
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:36.000
So, if Aristotle believed in demons, as most of the Greek philosophers, by the way, the word demon is a Greek word, yes, to whom is the Rambam referring?

c0063
00:21:36.000 --> 00:22:04.000
Now, I cannot prove it to you, but my claim is that he was referring to the Rambam, and the reason why I call him the Yevani is simply because it would have been very difficult for him, it was a matter of tactic, and more than anything else, if he would have referred to Rambam in this way, he would have found some opposition in the Jewish community.

c0064
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Now, why is it so important that there were demons, there must be demons? There is another theory of the Rambam, which is terribly important, in which he speaks about the Nisim Nistarim, Nisim Nistarim, and then later on, facing to Ladino, and also into Ladino literature, we speak about Milagros Ocultos, yes, the expression Milagros Ocultos, these are Nisim Nistarim.

c0065
00:22:33.000 --> 00:23:00.000
Nisim Nistarim, his theory is the following, that actually the whole world, yes, it is ruled according to some sort of esoteric system, yes, Milagros are continuous, and in one passage in which, yes, he mentions the Rambam, he says, I am surprised at the Rambam that minimizes the miracles and maximizes nature.

c0066
00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:25.000
And in another passage, Shelomo Benadrete, the Talmud, one of the famous students of the Rambam, he says like this, he is mentioning in a casual way, he mentions as well, you know, there are people who believe, and I am going to give you the quotation, and who believe in that, he is not a true Jew.

c0067
00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:48.000
And he says, and what is it? It is a passage from the Rambam, in the Morel Ebuchim, in which he says, there are certain things which according to nature are impossible, and therefore, of course, this rejects or contradicts the whole notion of Nisim Nistarim.

c0068
00:23:48.000 --> 00:24:02.000
So if there is Nisim Nistarim, and everything is ruled by some sort of a magical system to deny the existence of demons, it is simply heresy.

c0069
00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:17.000
And then the Rambam, again, everything I am saying is very, very well documented, he says explicitly that to deny demons is worse than the Egyptians, because the Egyptians at least believe in demons, and those Jews do not believe even in demons.

c0070
00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.000
One second, this is only one phase.

c0071
00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:31.000
Second point, which is terribly interesting, as a consequence of his ideology, the Rambam believed in astrology.

c0072
00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:53.000
The whole universe, yes, is controlled by celestial bodies, but lo and behold, these celestial bodies, they have some sort of a spirit, yes, which controls the celestial bodies, somehow the relationship of body and soul, or body and mind.

c0073
00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:58.000
Now, briefly, what is the theory of astrology?

c0074
00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:15.000
The theory of astrology is the following, that those spiritual things controlling the actual celestial bodies, if you build certain kind of images, yes, and what you see later on how you build those type of images,

c0075
00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:33.000
then those superior intelligence will sort of be fooled, yes, and they will think that instead of seeing the actual asters, or the actual celestial bodies, they will look into that image, and therefore they will act differently,

c0076
00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:51.000
so that the celestial bodies will continue with their own astronomical motion, but the intelligence of those astrological bodies will act differently, and therefore, here is the notion of astrology,

c0077
00:25:51.000 --> 00:26:11.000
and therefore you could change the course of history, yes, not by changing the mechanical motion of the bodies, the celestial bodies, but by so-called fooling, yes, that superior intelligence that controls those bodies.

c0078
00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:28.000
Now, you want to see the consequences of this, very interesting consequences, we all know that the Ramban was a physician, and according to some historians, he was a famous physician, a great physician,

c0079
00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:54.000
but I never read, but it's quite clear, that of course Ramban was a physician, but he practiced astrological medicine, basically the theory is the following, the zodiac power, somehow it is encoded in the human body,

c0080
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:18.000
and therefore, by knowing the right astrological signs, and how to manipulate those astrological signs, you could cure somebody, because of this, the Ramban used to build certain astrological images,

c0081
00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:42.000
example, if a person was sick, had some problem with the left kidney, would be the image of a virgin woman, this image has to be built by certain type of material, it has to be also built at certain zodiac, under a certain zodiac sign,

c0082
00:27:43.000 --> 00:28:01.000
it has to be offered certain type of incense, and then, when you place that image into the proper organ, that image will cure the sickness.

c0083
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:15.000
The Rashban brings the Ramban as the source for permitting the use of astrological images for medical purposes.

c0084
00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:36.000
Now, in this point, even some of the anti-Marmonidians were not ready to accept the use of images, as the famous Rabbi Sha'ul Abba Mare writes to the... Abba Mare, yes?

c0085
00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:52.000
You say Abba Mare, he says that if you want to permit, he writes in a very famous letter to the Rashban, by the way, we don't say Rashban, we say Rishba, yes?

c0086
00:28:52.000 --> 00:29:07.000
But, you know, in the famous letter to the Shlomovin Adrete, he says that if you want to permit the uses of such an image, then you have to permit the uses of all images.

c0087
00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:27.000
The answer that Rabbi Shlomovin Adrete gives is a little bit complex, I'm not going to go into the answer, but even the Me'iri, in the Me'iri in one place, very explicitly says that you're permitted to do anything to cure somebody, except you're not permitted to use astrological images for the purpose of medicine.

c0088
00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:29.000
The Me'iri says explicitly this.

c0089
00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:35.000
Now, let me summarize some very interesting points, we're going to have more interesting points.

c0090
00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:49.000
Well, there is a theory of what the Tora is, the Tora is a certain kind of knowledge, esoteric knowledge.

c0091
00:29:49.000 --> 00:30:08.000
This esoteric knowledge allows you to manipulate, discover, use the subtext of the Tora, which are the Shemot of God, yes, and through this you're permitted to do or you could do, yes, extraordinary things.

c0092
00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:33.000
This belief is based upon the notion that the whole universe is controlled by some sort of a supernatural system, and once that you could know and manipulate this supernatural system, of course, you could perform anything you do, including you could cure people, and the Ramban practiced this kind of astrological medicine.

c0093
00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:42.000
Now we can understand very well why he was not particularly satisfied with Maimonides' point that demons and so on, demonology, is just pure nonsense.

c0094
00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:50.000
Now, let me give you, I cannot go into the details, but I just want to give you an example from law, from halakha.

c0095
00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:56.000
Most scholars, when they deal with the ideas in Spain, they don't study the halakha sources.

c0096
00:30:57.000 --> 00:31:02.000
I want to give you one simple notion of halakha, which is so different than what we think the halakha is.

c0097
00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:08.000
And what is the Tora according to Ramban? What is the Tora according to Ramban? It's an interesting thing.

c0098
00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.000
The Gemara says,

c0099
00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:22.000
In Shabbat you are only permitted to read the Tora, and it is forbidden.

c0100
00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:33.000
And of course, they give you a reason why, because otherwise people would, instead of going to the best Midrash, they would study and read the Tora.

c0101
00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:37.000
That's a rabbinic enactment. Interesting.

c0102
00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:47.000
Are you permitted to read books of signs in Shabbat? Yes or no? Very interesting.

c0103
00:31:48.000 --> 00:32:00.000
Maimonides, in Pirush HaMishnayot, he says, No. Tora is one thing, Hokhma is another thing.

c0104
00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:07.000
And of course, Tora is the result of a covenant, etc.

c0105
00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:15.000
This question is presented to the Rashba, and the Rashba says, You are permitted to do, to read Hokhma.

c0106
00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:19.000
Because my teacher, the Ramban, permitted it.

c0107
00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:25.000
So here it is, what is Tora? Is Tora something, a covenant?

c0108
00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:29.000
Or is it a kind of knowledge? Is it a kind of Hokhma?

c0109
00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.000
And you see how this appears also in law.

c0110
00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:43.000
If you could say, Hokhma and Tora are of the same genetic order, there is no reason then to forbid Shifre Hokhma.

c0111
00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:49.000
Interesting, from the school of Gerona, Azariah of Gerona says,

c0112
00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:56.000
Kol Davah, says, whatever the Sehel produces, is Tora.

c0113
00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:01.000
Azariah of Gerona says, it's a revolutionary statement.

c0114
00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:08.000
In his famous Pirush HaMishnayot, he says, whatever the mind produces, that is Tora.

c0115
00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:19.000
So here we have actually two different concepts of Judaism, and with extraordinary implications.

c0116
00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:24.000
Now, it goes to the nicest part.

c0117
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:41.000
Of course, as we said before, the true Tora is not the Tora Rekanit, the empty Tora, according to the letter, like Paul.

c0118
00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:45.000
But the Tora that you know according to the Shemot.

c0119
00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:53.000
And in two places, not in one, in two places, the Rambam gives an example.

c0120
00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:58.000
And it is not a casual, it appears like a casual example, but it is more than a casual example.

c0121
00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:03.000
It says, an explanation, it says, how could you, for instance, read the Tora differently?

c0122
00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:08.000
And, we begin with Bereshit Bara Elohim et HaShamayim v'et HaAris.

c0123
00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:12.000
Bereshit Bara Elohim, at the beginning, God created, et cetera, et cetera.

c0124
00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:14.000
It says, you could read it differently.

c0125
00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:18.000
Bereshit, Berosh, Bara Elohim.

c0126
00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:23.000
At the beginning, God was created.

c0127
00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:28.000
God was created. Berosh, Bara Elohim.

c0128
00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:33.000
At the beginning, God was created.

c0129
00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:36.000
One second, one second.

c0130
00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:40.000
Incidentally, how do you divide the names of God?

c0131
00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:42.000
Those names, you know, the names of God.

c0132
00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:44.000
God has a name.

c0133
00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:46.000
He's 72 letters.

c0134
00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:48.000
And we want to see.

c0135
00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:53.000
This name is divided into words containing three.

c0136
00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:55.000
And you want to see why three?

c0137
00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:57.000
Three consonants.

c0138
00:34:58.000 --> 00:34:59.000
It's interesting.

c0139
00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:01.000
You want to see why?

c0140
00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:03.000
There is a She'elah.

c0141
00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:06.000
One important thing, historians sometimes don't look into She'elot Tuchuvot or the Pirushim.

c0142
00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:10.000
It's very important to understand, to look into the halakha and the way halakha was interpreted

c0143
00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:18.000
in order to understand the development of ideas within Judaism.

c0144
00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:21.000
There is an interesting She'elah.

c0145
00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:24.000
The She'elah was brought to Rabbi Shelomo ben Adrete.

c0146
00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:29.000
And the She'elah is, why do we say, in the prayers,

c0147
00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:36.000
Elohe Abraham, Elohe Yishak, Elohe Yaaqob, the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.

c0148
00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:38.000
Don't forget, we're in Christian Spain.

c0149
00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:46.000
We're in Christian Spain, 13th century, dense Christian atmosphere in Barcelona, imagine.

c0150
00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:50.000
Why do we have this?

c0151
00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:52.000
Any quotes?

c0152
00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:54.000
Quotes of theory.

c0153
00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:57.000
Approvingly.

c0154
00:35:57.000 --> 00:35:58.000
Approvingly.

c0155
00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:03.000
Saying, I'm going to answer you this question with another question.

c0156
00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:06.000
How can we possibly bless God?

c0157
00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:09.000
How could you say, you know.

c0158
00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:14.000
So it says, take the letter, the word Baruch.

c0159
00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:16.000
Yes?

c0160
00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:20.000
The three consonants, Bet, Resh, Chav.

c0161
00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:25.000
Well, it says, this you could read it in three different ways.

c0162
00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:31.000
One way, you read it as Bechor, first born.

c0163
00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:40.000
The other, as Rocheb, the one who's mounting, the mounted one.

c0164
00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:44.000
And the third, as Kerub.

c0165
00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:54.000
Now, and the three, Bechor, Rocheb, Kerub, are one, Baruch.

c0166
00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:59.000
Now, it describes what the Bechor is.

c0167
00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.000
The Bechor is that aspect of God, which is Mashgiach ve-Yosef.

c0168
00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:09.000
Yosef u-Mashgiach.

c0169
00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:12.000
All right?

c0170
00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:16.000
This is the first one, the Bechor, God as a firstborn.

c0171
00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:18.000
All right?

c0172
00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:24.000
The Rocheb is another aspect of God.

c0173
00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:29.000
And the Kerub, something like the Holy Ghost.

c0174
00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:31.000
All right?

c0175
00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:36.000
Now, interesting, these three ones are one.

c0176
00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:39.000
Of course, I connect this, I may be wrong.

c0177
00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:46.000
Part of the theory that, if we're going to say that Bereshit means Baruch is Barai Elohim,

c0178
00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.000
then you could understand the whole notion of Bechor.

c0179
00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:55.000
Now, what is important to remember is that we are not speaking in the United States, 1992,

c0180
00:37:56.000 --> 00:38:00.000
but we are in Catalonia, 13th century.

c0181
00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:12.000
Within that context, I would say it was almost impossible not to associate or to identify this with the Trinity.

c0182
00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:17.000
As a matter of fact, traditionally speaking, you can never describe God as a Bechor.

c0183
00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:25.000
The first time that we have the Jewish God described as a Bechor was by Paul in the New Testament,

c0184
00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:30.000
where Jesus is explicitly described as the firstborn.

c0185
00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:45.000
Interesting enough, with the same attributes as Yosef and Mashgiach, exactly like that of the Rashba of Shelomo ben Adrete.

c0186
00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:55.000
Some parallel ideas also you find among the famous Rabi Azariel de Gerona.

c0187
00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:58.000
And he says, what does it mean the word Amen?

c0188
00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:04.000
Yosef switches it. Amen means Oman, a moon, and Amen.

c0189
00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:07.000
All right, three things, you know, and the three days are one.

c0190
00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:16.000
And it's part of a theory of which the first three sefirot, as I have more important, hierarchically superior,

c0191
00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:21.000
I would say genetically different than the other seven sefirot.

c0192
00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:30.000
Now, you must remember, yes, that again, we are living in a Christian environment.

c0193
00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:33.000
I just want to mention one more point.

c0194
00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:36.000
And I bring it in my new book.

c0195
00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:49.000
There is a, even Verga brings a dispute, fictional probably, between a Jew and a Christian.

c0196
00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:53.000
And the Christian says, well, why don't you convert?

c0197
00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:57.000
And the Jew says, well, you know, one God.

c0198
00:39:58.000 --> 00:39:59.000
He says, no.

c0199
00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:07.000
And he quotes, he says, according to your Rabbi, I say, Hachmeyashkenaz, he mentions Hachmeyashkenaz, he says very explicitly, yes,

c0200
00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:14.000
the belief, the true monotheism is the Trinity.

c0201
00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:27.000
And then I ask you, the Christian to the Jew, whom you should rely on, your opinion or that of the preceding Rabbis?

c0202
00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:30.000
Let me summarize.

c0203
00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:36.000
For centuries, Jews lived in Spain.

c0204
00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:45.000
And they had some sort of theory, never explicit, of what Judaism was, a covenant.

c0205
00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:59.000
The authority for Judaism, a bedim, a Jewish court, going all the way, whether historically true or not, irrelevant to Moses.

c0206
00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:11.000
And those institutions had defined, and every mizvah was mazbuta, as Yehuda ha-Levi says, what the Tora is.

c0207
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:22.000
The Tora is not the result of speculation, ishtihad, or chias, analogy, certain traditions.

c0208
00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:30.000
Now, contrast this with a new theory.

c0209
00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:34.000
First of all, the Rabbis have no authority.

c0210
00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:38.000
This terminated the authority with the destruction of the Temple.

c0211
00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:45.000
Second, the real Tora is the subtext, not what you're reading.

c0212
00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.000
There is an esoteric subtext.

c0213
00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:53.000
As a matter of fact, the esoteric aspect of the Tora is Tora Rekanit.

c0214
00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:59.000
And this gives you power.

c0215
00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:05.000
You can control all kind of phenomena.

c0216
00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:15.000
Part of this theory proposes not only the existence of demons, that's not my opinion, it's neutral, it doesn't mean anything.

c0217
00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:26.000
But, that God, as was traditionally known in Judaism, is not the real God.

c0218
00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:34.000
As a matter of fact, later on, the student of Shabbat HaTzevi, there's one famous card also, in which he has the first thing, he says,

c0219
00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:38.000
It is heresy to pray to Sibat HaSibod.

c0220
00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:41.000
It is forbidden to pray to real God.

c0221
00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:44.000
What you have to pray is to the other God.

c0222
00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:48.000
This is Hayun headed, and Cardoso headed.

c0223
00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:53.000
Because that other God, somehow, I don't know, you cannot reach.

c0224
00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:56.000
Bereshit is Bara Elohim.

c0225
00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:05.000
And to that Elohim, which is the Bechor, he is the one who controls, you could address yourself.

c0226
00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:17.000
Moreover, images that were forever a taboo in Jewish life, are accepted under some circumstances.

c0227
00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:29.000
I propose that this type of theory, on the one hand, shook some of the more traditional elements of Judaism,

c0228
00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:39.000
and of course, inadvertently, promoted some sort of intellectual or spiritual sympathy towards Christianity.

c0229
00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:41.000
Thank you very much.

c0230
00:43:52.000 --> 00:43:53.000
Questions?

c0231
00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.000
Other questions?

c0232
00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:04.000
Yes.

c0233
00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:08.000
Would you identify yourself?

c0234
00:44:08.000 --> 00:44:09.000
Identify yourself.

c0235
00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:24.000
The opposition between what you call the discovery of the truth on one side,

c0236
00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:29.000
and the creation of truth or of text on the other side,

c0237
00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:38.000
is some kind of discovery of the 13th century,

c0238
00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:44.000
and these parallel contemporary to the emergence of qabbala,

c0239
00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:58.000
and that all these things are somehow related in a contemporary, contextual, chronological framework?

c0240
00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:16.000
Or are we saying that this convergence of different concepts was casual in the 13th century,

c0241
00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:21.000
in the Nahmanides context,

c0242
00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:29.000
and then it was a case, it was fortuitous, that this kind of perception arose?

c0243
00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.000
Yes.

c0244
00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:33.000
Exoterically, I agree with the latter part.

c0245
00:45:34.000 --> 00:45:35.000
Esoterically, with the first part.

c0246
00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:59.000
The Tora, in my opinion, cannot be related not only to Nahmanides, but to any of his students.

c0247
00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.000
This attribution is very late, found only in the 15th or 16th century,

c0248
00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:16.000
and it has to do with a secondary elite, someone, a Castilian Kabbalist in the group of Chikatilo.

c0249
00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:21.000
So, I would like to say that this Tora has nothing parallel in the Nahmanides school,

c0250
00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:29.000
but it's part of another qabbala school which must be studied in itself,

c0251
00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.000
and that part is a secondary elite, not Nahmanides.

c0252
00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:39.000
After a long discussion we had this summer, I looked into the Rashba story,

c0253
00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:43.000
and I agree with you that we have there Ashkenaz material,

c0254
00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:48.000
and we can bring much more material, no question.

c0255
00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:54.000
However, the solution for the question, what is going on there?

c0256
00:46:55.000 --> 00:47:00.000
We have a Christology, a hidden Christology, coming from Ashkenaz sources,

c0257
00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:03.000
from any other type of sources you like, or not.

c0258
00:47:04.000 --> 00:47:09.000
It's a question which is very difficult, because we have already in the Midrash Tehillim

c0259
00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:15.000
discussions which can be easily called Christological.

c0260
00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:23.000
And these discussions reverberate in Ashkenaz and in Spain altogether.

c0261
00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:37.000
In any way, I didn't see in the qabbala of Nahmadinian school an emphasis or something like that.

c0262
00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:42.000
It is quoted not from a qabbala source.

c0263
00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:45.000
The Rashba quotes someone else.

c0264
00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:50.000
That's why I would say Nahmanides school moved in one direction,

c0265
00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:54.000
which I wouldn't say is so Christologically oriented,

c0266
00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:58.000
while in Castile we have much more influence.

c0267
00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:04.000
In the Zohar, we have much more Christological orientation.

c0268
00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:14.000
So that's another, I would like not to say now, Nahmanides, he doesn't need my apology,

c0269
00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:19.000
but Rashba, but it seems to me, speaking historically,

c0270
00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:24.000
their qabbala is different from this point of view.

c0271
00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:31.000
There are a lot of other types of qabbala which were closer and even influenced by Christological views.

c0272
00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:39.000
That's to say, I would like to define a little bit more, I'd say, the distribution.

c0273
00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:48.000
The secondary elite was easily absorbing Christian influence in Castile,

c0274
00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:52.000
and not so much in the school of Nahmanides.

c0275
00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:55.000
And that's right, we have a quotation.

c0276
00:48:56.000 --> 00:48:59.000
But it's a question someone asked Rashba, and he quotes.

c0277
00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:03.000
And his answer is, whatever he answers, I'd like not to enter, it's a very long story,

c0278
00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:07.000
but it's not his vision that's proposed there.

c0279
00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:11.000
That he is pushed by someone to address a certain issue.

c0280
00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:17.000
So I would push towards a much more detailed differentiation

c0281
00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:21.000
between different forms of qabbala in different places.

c0282
00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:26.000
Unfortunately, we don't have an history of qabbala which can rely to see what was said when.

c0283
00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:29.000
But if we are going to do something like that sometime,

c0284
00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:37.000
in my opinion, Castile is much more open to Christian influence rather than Catalonia.

c0285
00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:41.000
And Catalonia had a little bit earlier in the Geronese qabbala, they were influenced,

c0286
00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:46.000
but Nahmanides moved away from such a view.

c0287
00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:49.000
I would like to...

c0288
00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:54.000
First of all, it's always a pleasure to listen to Professor Edam.

c0289
00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:56.000
It's a pleasure, right?

c0290
00:49:57.000 --> 00:50:01.000
But I want to make two points about the comments of Professor Edam.

c0291
00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:05.000
Number one, you know, talking about Sephardim and not Sephardim,

c0292
00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:10.000
you know, this is celebrating or remembering the 500th anniversary.

c0293
00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:15.000
You scratch a Sephardim, and you find a businessman underneath.

c0294
00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:16.000
All right?

c0295
00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:18.000
It's the truth.

c0296
00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:20.000
We were the people of the business.

c0297
00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:25.000
And I presented, I packaged my thesis in advance.

c0298
00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:28.000
Part of business is packaging.

c0299
00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:34.000
And that's why I asked Professor Edam whether the difference between the school of Gerona

c0300
00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:40.000
and the school of Catalonia was a question of strategy or something more.

c0301
00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:44.000
That is one point that I think has to be clarified.

c0302
00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:46.000
First of all.

c0303
00:50:47.000 --> 00:50:48.000
Second, a major point.

c0304
00:50:49.000 --> 00:50:54.000
And this was mentioned by Rene Girard, which I think is a great, great contemporary thinker.

c0305
00:50:55.000 --> 00:50:56.000
This is about differences.

c0306
00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:02.000
You know, from within a system, people only see the differences.

c0307
00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:07.000
From the outside, everything seems the same.

c0308
00:51:08.000 --> 00:51:13.000
In other words, the differences that you're presenting between the school of Gerona

c0309
00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:16.000
and Catalonia, they're true.

c0310
00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:18.000
From within, there are differences.

c0311
00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:25.000
But when you see it from the outside, it's very difficult to distinguish.

c0312
00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:28.000
And last but not least, part of the packaging.

c0313
00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:34.000
From my point of view, I really am not concerned.

c0314
00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:39.000
I don't care what the Somoven Adrete, the Ramban, etc. really believed in.

c0315
00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:47.000
My concern is with the impact of such a doctrine

c0316
00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:53.000
within the semantic environment of Christian faith.

c0317
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:57.000
And I say, granting everything that you're saying,

c0318
00:51:58.000 --> 00:52:02.000
still within that particular semantic environment,

c0319
00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:06.000
anybody who opens the Somoven Adrete and reads,

c0320
00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:11.000
Bechor, that God is a Bechor, Kerub, Rohem,

c0321
00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:19.000
it is simply impossible not to identify this with some sort of a trinity.

c0322
00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:22.000
When you're reading the Hagdama of the Ramban,

c0323
00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:28.000
and you say, and you read, Berosh, Idvara, Elohim,

c0324
00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:31.000
no matter what kind of Jew you are,

c0325
00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:34.000
if you are in a Christian environment,

c0326
00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:39.000
then you must somehow support a notion of something parallel

c0327
00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:43.000
to the Christian doctrine of Jesus, and so on, etc.

c0328
00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:49.000
So I'm talking very subjectively within the context of that period,

c0329
00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:52.000
not what really the Ramban actually believed in,

